# Prebuilt Layer for Jam-Based Frameworks

Architecture concept. Platinum (Pt) is the reference consumer. The same model
applies to any Jam project that must ship third-party libraries without putting
Conan or vcpkg on the user's path.

---

## 1. Purpose

Jam remains a build system, not a package-manager frontend. A user needs Jam,
a compiler, and optionally Fetch. Conan runs only in CI, where it produces
relocatable ABI prefixes.

Three supply paths exist in parallel. None replaces the others.

1. System / toolchain sysroot:
     Linux, Yocto, manufacturer sysroot, Homebrew
2. Prebuilt prefix:
     Windows / macOS without system zlib, png, jpeg, FreeType, OpenSSL
3. Vendor / inline sources:
     builds without external dependencies, same flags as the project

Configure flags and env vars (`ZLIB_INCLUDES=...`, `--with-xxx-dir`) override
whichever of the three is in use, and they always win.

The prebuilt prefix is a **hint source** for the existing per-package
Configure rules. It is not a compiler sysroot and it does not mutate global
`C++FLAGS` or `LINKFLAGS`.

---

## 2. Configure priority

The cascade depends on whether a prebuilt prefix was selected.

**Prebuilt not selected** (no `--with-prebuilt` / `--fetch-prebuilt`):

1. User paths / env / `ZLIB_LINKLIBS=...`
2. System: pkg-config and compile/link probes (`ZLib-Configure` with empty hints)
3. Vendor / inline sources

**Prebuilt selected:**

1. User paths / env / `--with-inline-xxx` — still always win
2. Prebuilt prefix, injected as the same five hints `*-Configure` already
   accepts
3. Vendor / inline sources, if the prebuilt probe fails

There is no unhinted first probe when the prebuilt is selected. On POSIX,
`ZLib-Configure` with empty arguments finds `/usr/include/zlib.h` and `-lz`.
A second "then try the prebuilt" step would never run, and prebuilt png would
be linked against system zlib.

There is no silent fallback between two prebuilt ABIs. If the chosen prefix
is missing: error, or vendor, reported clearly.

`--with-prebuilt` names a prefix that must already exist (default cache, or
`=<root>`). `--fetch-prebuilt` is the same load, after downloading that
prefix into the default cache. Neither changes the meaning of user overrides.

---

## 3. What a prebuilt prefix is

Not `full_deploy/host/zlib/1.3.1/Release/...`.
Not `SetDependencyPath`.
Not generated `-I` / `-L` / `-l` text.
Not a global include or lib path applied to every translation unit.

A prebuilt prefix is one **relocatable ABI tree**: identity, a package graph,
and on-disk artifacts. The consumer maps package names to the framework
variables `ZLIB_INCLUDES`, `ZLIB_C++FLAGS`, `ZLIB_LINKLIBS`, `ZLIB_LIBDIR`,
`ZLIB_LINKFLAGS` (and the PNG / JPEG / FREETYPE / SSL equivalents). Existing
`*-Configure` rules then probe those hints.

Identity:

```text
<os>-<arch>-<toolset>[-<abi>]
windows-x64-msvc-md
windows-x64-msvc-mdd
macos-arm64-clang
linux-x64-clang
yocto-cortexa7-gcc
```

A prebuilt may export several ABIs. Each ABI is a complete prefix, not a
`debug/lib` folder inside another prefix.

Windows currently has two ABIs because `/MD` and `/MDd` are two MSVC runtimes
(`RuntimeLibrary`, `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL`), not "zlib less optimized".
Linux and macOS typically have one. Later splits (ASan, `mt`) are the same
mechanism: extra artifact, same layout.

### 3.1 A single exported ABI has no name

Do not call the only ABI `default`, `common`, or `standard`. Those words
collide with "the ABI Configure picks" and they age badly: the day ASan
appears, `standard` plus `asan` is a false pair.

The ABI tag exists to distinguish **variants of the same**
`<os>-<arch>-<toolset>`. If there is only one variant, the three-part
identity is already unique. Omit the tag from the directory and the zip
name.

- One prefix: `macos-arm64-clang`, no ABI component
- Split later: keep `macos-arm64-clang` for the original; add
  `macos-arm64-clang-asan`. Do not rename the original to `...-standard`
- Windows always has a tag (`md`, `mdd`) because it always has two

`CONFIG` (project debug/release) and prebuilt ABI are different axes.
Mapping is a prebuilt convention, not a universal debug/release pair:

- Project release: Windows `md`; other OS the one untagged prefix
- Project debug: Windows `mdd`; other OS the same untagged prefix

Auto-map only where the prebuilt actually has a debug ABI.

---

## 4. Layout of an ABI prefix

Packages live in **per-package directories** under one ABI prefix. A flat
`include/` + `lib/` sysroot would only pay off if the compiler saw the prefix
globally. That is the model this design rejects.

```text
<prefix>/                          # one ABI, one zip, one contract file
  jam/
    packages.jam                   # data, no flags, no rules
  zlib/
    include/zlib.h
    lib/zlib.lib                   # or libz.a / libz.dylib
    bin/zlib.dll                   # omitted for static
  png/
    include/png.h
    lib/png16.lib
    bin/libpng16.dll
  jpeg/
    include/...
    lib/...
    bin/...
  freetype/
    include/ft2build.h
    include/freetype2/...          # extra dir allowed
    lib/freetype.lib
    bin/freetype.dll
  openssl/
    include/openssl/...
    lib/libssl.lib libcrypto.lib
    bin/...
```

Directory names match `PREBUILT_PACKAGES` (`zlib`, `png`, `jpeg`,
`freetype`, `openssl`), not Conan recipe names (`libpng`, `libjpeg-turbo`).

Default locations, relative to the prefix:

- Headers: `$(pkg)/include`
- Import libs / `.a`: `$(pkg)/lib`
- Deploy: `$(pkg)/bin`

`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_INCLUDES.$(pkg)` exists only when that convention is not enough
(FreeType's `include/freetype2`). `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.$(pkg)` lists
filenames in that package's `bin/` (`zlib.dll`), not prefix-relative paths.

One zip per ABI. Not one zip per package (that would be a package manager).
`md` and `mdd` are two prefixes, never two trees in one zip.

No version directories, no `full_deploy`, no `debug/` inside `md`.
Library names inside a given prefix are stable (`zlib.lib` in both `md` and
`mdd`). Configure does not guess `zlib` / `zlibd` / `z` on the prebuilt path.

Relocatable: no absolute paths in the zip. The consumer sets `PREBUILT_ROOT`
before including `jam/packages.jam`.

Optional: `$(pkg)/lib/pkgconfig/` as a bonus. Jam does not need it.

---

## 5. `jam/packages.jam` — data only

Jam syntax, **one file per ABI prefix**, at `<prefix>/jam/packages.jam`.
Variables only: no schema engine, no rules, no toolset flags. Logic lives
in the consumer (`jam/prebuilt.jam` in the framework). The file is writable
by hand and readable without running Jam. Jam remains the canonical form.

Prefix-wide identity uses `PREBUILT_*`. Anything that varies by package
uses `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_*` plus a dotted package name.

### 5.1 One file, not one file per package

There is no `zlib/package.jam`. Partial *use* of the prefix does not need
it. Partial *shipping* (a zip that contains only zlib) is a package manager
and is out of scope.

What "only part of the prebuilt" already means:

- The zip still contains the whole prefix. One identity, one ABI, one
  fetch.
- `Jamconfigure` only calls `Prebuilt-Includes zlib` (and friends) for
  packages it actually configures. Unused directories sit unused.
- `--with-inline-png` still skips the png hints and builds inline png
  against prebuilt zlib, or the other way around, through the existing
  per-package blocks.

What a per-package `package.jam` would add: a load graph. Including png's
file would have to pull zlib's file because `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.png = zlib`.
That is dependency resolution at include time. This layer does not solve
dependencies.

`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS` is a **declared closed graph**, written by the producer,
not a solver. The consumer walks it for include closure (always), deploy
closure (shared DLLs next to the exe), and link closure (static only). No
versions, no optional deps, no "find zlib elsewhere". If png is in the
prefix, zlib is in the prefix.

Identity (`PREBUILT_ID`, `PREBUILT_LINKAGE`) and the graph belong in one
place. Splitting them across package files would duplicate the ABI on every
file and make a hand-written prefix worse, not better.

### 5.2 Indexed names (`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.$(pkg)`)

Jam has no maps. A variable whose name contains a dot is still one
variable: `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.zlib` is the literal name. Indexing is

```jam
$(PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.$(pkg))    # pkg = zlib  →  PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.zlib
```

That is the idiomatic Jam dictionary (`HDRRULE.$(target)`, and so on). It
is a good idea here.

Not used:

- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS_zlib` — works (`$(PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS_$(pkg))`) but reads as a
  different family from `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS`
- Parallel lists (`PREBUILT_PACKAGES` plus a positional `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS`) —
  fragile when a package is omitted
- Putting `FLinkLibs` or paths in the file — toolset and relocatable root
  belong in the consumer

Dots are not a struct operator. Do not write `$(PREBUILT.LIBS.zlib)`.

### 5.3 Example

```jam
# <prefix>/jam/packages.jam
if ! $(PREBUILT_PACKAGES_JAM_INCLUDED) {
PREBUILT_PACKAGES_JAM_INCLUDED = true ;

PREBUILT_SCHEMA  = 1 ;
PREBUILT_ID      = windows-x64-msvc-md ;
PREBUILT_LINKAGE = shared ;

PREBUILT_PACKAGES = zlib png jpeg freetype openssl ;

PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.zlib        = zlib ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.zlib      = zlib.dll ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_VERSION.zlib     = 1.3.1 ;

PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.png         = png16 ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.png      = zlib ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.png       = libpng16.dll ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_VERSION.png      = 1.6.43 ;

PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.jpeg        = jpeg ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.jpeg      = jpeg62.dll ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_VERSION.jpeg     = 3.0.3 ;

PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.freetype    = freetype ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.freetype = png zlib ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.freetype  = freetype.dll ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_VERSION.freetype = 2.13.2 ;

PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.openssl     = ssl crypto ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS.openssl  = crypt32 ws2_32 advapi32 user32 ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.openssl   = libssl-3-x64.dll libcrypto-3-x64.dll ;
PREBUILT_PACKAGE_VERSION.openssl  = 3.3.1 ;

}
```

A macOS prefix uses a three-part `PREBUILT_ID` (`macos-arm64-clang`).

Prefix-wide variables that earn their place:

- `PREBUILT_SCHEMA`: contract version. Cheap, lets the consumer reject an
  unknown file.
- `PREBUILT_ID`: the identity string, same as the directory and zip name.
  Echo and a mismatch check (`--fetch-prebuilt=windows-x64-msvc-md` pointed
  at an `mdd` tree). Not used to invent compiler flags.
- `PREBUILT_LINKAGE`: `shared` or `static`. This is the one identity field
  `Prebuilt-LinkLibs` / `Prebuilt-Deploy` branch on (link closure, whether
  deploy files exist). If every shipped prefix is shared, it is still
  worth stating: a hand-written static prefix must not be treated as
  shared.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGES`: which dotted keys exist. Do not scan the disk.

Not in the file — denormalized copies of `PREBUILT_ID`, of Jam, or of
product policy:

- `PREBUILT_OS` / `PREBUILT_ARCH` / `PREBUILT_TOOLSET`: Jam already has
  `TARGET_OS`, `TARGET_OSPLAT`, `TOOLSET`. The compile probe fails if the
  prefix does not match. Parsing `PREBUILT_ID` is enough if a check is
  ever needed.
- `PREBUILT_ABI`: already the optional fourth component of `PREBUILT_ID`.
  Configure picked the prefix before `packages.jam` is read.
- `PREBUILT_CRT` (`MD` / `MDd`): duplicate of the Windows ABI tag. The
  project already has `/MD` or `/MDd`; the zip must not set them.
- `PREBUILT_PUBLIC_ABI` (`c`): a product rule ("no STL in the prefix"),
  not a Configure input.
- `PREBUILT_REDIST`: `mdd` is not redistributable. That is a Windows fact
  the framework already knows from the ABI tag, not data in the zip.

### 5.4 Variable families

- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.$(pkg)`: artifact names inside the prefix.
  Not `-lssl`, `ssl.lib`, `libssl.lib`.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.$(pkg)`: other packages in this prefix (the graph).
  Not the link line, not a solver.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS.$(pkg)`: linker names of the OS, not in the zip.
  Not `-lpthread` as a flag.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_FRAMEWORKS.$(pkg)`: macOS framework names.
  Not `-framework Security`.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEFINES.$(pkg)`: token, not `-D`.
  Not raw `C++FLAGS`.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_INCLUDES.$(pkg)`: paths relative to the prefix, only if not
  `$(pkg)/include`. Not `-I`.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.$(pkg)`: filenames in `$(pkg)/bin`.
  Not prefix-relative paths, not a global list.
- `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_VERSION.$(pkg)`: documentation / manifest.
  Not a search path.

Omit empty lists. No `PREBUILT_ROOT` in the file. No `FLinkLibs`, no
`C++FLAGS +=`, no `-L`, no `/LIBPATH`.

Do not write `ZLIB_LINKLIBS` / `SSL_LINKLIBS` into the zip. Mapping package
to consumer variable is the framework's job. The zip knows `zlib` / `png` /
`openssl`. A MinGW prefix and an MSVC prefix differ in names and `syslibs`,
not in flag syntax.

The `mdd` prefix: `PREBUILT_ID = windows-x64-msvc-mdd`, same linkage,
different `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY` list.

---

## 6. Include closure vs. link closure

pkg-config abstracts over unknown distros. Here the package set is closed and
the layout is known. This is not a pkg-config clone.

Three things pkg-config mixes — kept separate:

- Libs in the prefix (`freetype`, `png16`, `zlib`):
  `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS` / `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS`
- OS system libs (`crypt32`, `ws2_32`):
  `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS` — names; Jam applies `FLinkLibs`
- Real flags (`-pthread`, `-framework`, `/DEFAULTLIB:`):
  consumer / toolset, not the zip

`pthread` stays `PTHREAD_LINKLIBS` from Configure. The prefix may store the
name `pthread`, never `-lpthread`.

**Includes are always transitive.** `png.h` includes `zlib.h`. `Pt-Gfx` does
`SubDirHdrs $(PNG_INCLUDES)` and does not add `ZLIB_INCLUDES`. Therefore
`Prebuilt-Includes png` returns `png/include` **and** `zlib/include`.
That compile closure follows `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS` regardless of shared vs.
static. Walking `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS` is closure of a known DAG, not resolving
a dependency.

**Deploy files are per package.** There is no `PREBUILT_DEPLOY_FILES`.
`png.h` needs `zlib.h` at compile time; `libpng16.dll` needs `zlib.dll` next
to the exe. Staging therefore walks `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS` for deploy
too, even when the link line does not (shared). Static packages omit
`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY`. The consumer concatenates when an app wants
every DLL of the prefix; the zip does not pre-flatten that list.

**Link lines are not always transitive.**

- Shared, Linux/mac, C ABI: direct `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS`
- Shared, OpenSSL, MSVC: direct `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS` + `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS`
- Static: closure over `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS`, then all `libs` + `syslibs`
- Framework as shared lib: apps link the framework; prefix DLLs are runtime
- Framework as static lib: closure one step further onto the app link

Shared `.dll` / `.so` / `.dylib` already record NEEDED. Dumping
`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS` onto the command line is overlinking. `Pt-Gfx` already
passes `ZLIB_LINKLIBS` separately from `PNG_LINKLIBS`.

MSVC import libs: shared OpenSSL still needs `crypt32 ws2_32 advapi32 user32`
on the line. That is why `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS` exists.

`FIncludes` and `FLibPath` expand lists (`-I$(<)`, `/LIBPATH:$(<)`).
`CheckDependencyLinkC++` already takes `libdir *`. Several include dirs and
several lib dirs on one package are valid.

---

## 7. Consumer API (`jam/prebuilt.jam`)

Not in the zip. Once per project. This is the framework file; the prefix
file is `jam/packages.jam`.

There is **no** `Prebuilt-Apply`. A rule that does

```jam
C++FLAGS  += [ FIncludes [ FDirName $(PREBUILT_ROOT) include ] ] ;
LINKFLAGS += [ FLibPath  [ FDirName $(PREBUILT_ROOT) lib     ] ] ;
```

would put prebuilt headers and lib paths on every translation unit. That
fights `SubDirHdrs $(ZLIB_INCLUDES)`, `--with-inline-zlib` (`ZLIB_INCLUDES`
→ `src/zlib` while global `-I` still points at the prefix), and mixed
sources (system png + prebuilt zlib). Jamfiles already consume per-package
variables:

- `*_INCLUDES`: `SubDirHdrs` in modules that use the package
- `*_C++FLAGS`: `ObjectC++Flags` on specific sources
- `*_LINKLIBS`: `LinkLibs` on the target
- `*_LINKFLAGS`: `LinkFlags` on the target (`FLibPath` is already inside)
- `*_LIBDIR`: Configure-time search hint only

The prefix therefore fills that same five-tuple through rules. Toolset
helpers stay in the consumer: `FLinkLibs`, `FIncludes`, `FDefines`,
`FLibPath`.

```jam
rule Prebuilt-Import ( root )
{
    PREBUILT_ROOT = $(root) ;
    include [ FDirName $(PREBUILT_ROOT) jam packages.jam ] ;
}

rule Prebuilt-Includes ( packages + )
{
    # Direct $(pkg)/include (or PREBUILT_PACKAGE_INCLUDES.$(pkg)),
    # plus compile closure over PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.
}

rule Prebuilt-CxxFlags ( packages + )
{
    # [ FDefines $(PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEFINES.$(pkg)) ] — empty for the current C packages
}

rule Prebuilt-LinkLibs ( packages + )
{
    # Shared: PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS + PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS of the named packages.
    # Static: same after Prebuilt-Closure.
    # Always: [ FLinkLibs ... ], never raw flags from the zip.
}

rule Prebuilt-LibDir ( packages + )
{
    # $(root)/$(pkg)/lib for the named packages
    # (and their needs, when the link probe must see them).
}

rule Prebuilt-LinkFlags ( packages + )
{
    # Extra flags only. Do not duplicate FLibPath here:
    # CheckDependencyLinkC++ already does FLibPath $(libdir).
}

rule Prebuilt-Deploy ( packages + )
{
    # $(root)/$(pkg)/bin/$(PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.$(pkg))
    # plus deploy closure over PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.
}
```

`Prebuilt-Import` only sets `PREBUILT_ROOT` and includes `jam/packages.jam`.
It does not touch `C++FLAGS`, `LINKFLAGS`, or `ZLIB_*`.

---

## 8. Integration with `Jamconfigure`

The existing contract of every `*-Configure` rule is:

```text
*-Configure INCLUDES : C++FLAGS : LINKLIBS : LIBDIR : LINKFLAGS
```

If any hint is set, pkg-config is skipped and the first link attempt is the
caller-provided libs. If all hints are empty, pkg-config and name guessing
(`zlibd` / `zlib` / `z`) run. Side effects on success: the five `*_`
variables.

The prebuilt prefix must look like a user who set those variables on the
command line. It must not look like a second search space (`DEPS_INCLUDES` /
`DEPS_LIBDIR`).

### 8.1 Load the prefix, do not apply it

The legacy `--with-platform-sdk` flow fetches an archive and `include`s a generated
`platform-sdk-release.jam` / `platform-sdk-debug.jam` that assigns `ZLIB_*`
directly and still encodes `full_deploy/host/...` paths.

Replace that include with a load of `jam/packages.jam`. `--with-prebuilt`
or `--fetch-prebuilt` selects the layer. Fetch runs only for
`--fetch-prebuilt`, and only into the default cache. Mapping `CONFIG` →
`md` / `mdd` happens here, not inside the zip.

```jam
# After toolset setup, before the zlib / png / ... blocks.
# Selected if --with-prebuilt or --fetch-prebuilt is present (bare or =value).
PT_PREBUILT_ID ?= [ GetOption --with-prebuilt ] ;
PT_FETCH_PREBUILT_ID ?= [ GetOption --fetch-prebuilt ] ;

if --with-prebuilt in $(ARGV) || $(PT_PREBUILT_ID) ||
   --fetch-prebuilt in $(ARGV) || $(PT_FETCH_PREBUILT_ID)
{
  PT_PREBUILT_ID ?= [ Prebuilt-Tuple $(abi) ] ;
  PT_PREBUILT_ROOT = [ FDirName $(PT_TOP) prebuilt $(PT_PREBUILT_ID) ] ;
  Prebuilt-Import $(PT_PREBUILT_ROOT) ;
}
```

Do not `include` a file that writes `ZLIB_INCLUDES = ...`.

### 8.2 zlib — one `ZLib-Configure`, prebuilt as `?=` hints

Today:

```jam
Load zlib.jam ;

if --with-inline-zlib in $(ARGV)
{
    WITH_BUILTIN_ZLIB = yes ;
}

if ! $(WITH_BUILTIN_ZLIB)
{
    WITH_ZLIB = [ ZLib-Configure $(ZLIB_INCLUDES) : $(ZLIB_C++FLAGS) :
                                 $(ZLIB_LINKLIBS) : $(ZLIB_LIBDIR) : $(ZLIB_LINKFLAGS) ] ;
}

if ! $(WITH_ZLIB)
{
    WITH_BUILTIN_ZLIB = yes ;
}
```

With the prebuilt selected, fill **only unset** hints, then call Configure
**once**:

```jam
Load zlib.jam ;

if --with-inline-zlib in $(ARGV)
{
    WITH_BUILTIN_ZLIB = yes ;
}

if ! $(WITH_BUILTIN_ZLIB)
{
    if $(PREBUILT_ROOT)
    {
        ZLIB_INCLUDES  ?= [ Prebuilt-Includes zlib ] ;
        ZLIB_C++FLAGS  ?= [ Prebuilt-CxxFlags zlib ] ;
        ZLIB_LINKLIBS  ?= [ Prebuilt-LinkLibs zlib ] ;
        ZLIB_LIBDIR    ?= [ Prebuilt-LibDir zlib ] ;
        ZLIB_LINKFLAGS ?= [ Prebuilt-LinkFlags zlib ] ;
    }

    WITH_ZLIB = [ ZLib-Configure $(ZLIB_INCLUDES) : $(ZLIB_C++FLAGS) :
                                 $(ZLIB_LINKLIBS) : $(ZLIB_LIBDIR) : $(ZLIB_LINKFLAGS) ] ;
}

if ! $(WITH_ZLIB)
{
    WITH_BUILTIN_ZLIB = yes ;
}
```

Why `?=`, and why a single call:

- `ZLIB_INCLUDES=C:/third-party/zlib` on the command line stays in force.
- `--with-inline-zlib` still skips Configure entirely.
- Without `PREBUILT_ROOT`, the block is unchanged: empty hints, system
  probe, then vendor.
- With `PREBUILT_ROOT`, hints are set **before** the only `ZLib-Configure`.
  pkg-config is skipped. The first link attempt is `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.zlib`.
  System `/usr/include/zlib.h` does not win.
- A two-step "probe system, then prebuilt" is wrong whenever the prebuilt
  was requested: the first probe succeeds on POSIX.

The probe that remains is "does this prefix match this compiler?", not
"search the machine for zlib".

`*_LIBDIR` is required. Setting only `*_LINKLIBS` leaves the linker without
`FLibPath` of the package's `lib/` directory. `CheckDependencyLinkC++`
turns `LIBDIR` into `*_LINKFLAGS`.

### 8.3 png, jpeg, FreeType — same pattern

```jam
Load libpng.jam ;

if --with-inline-libpng in $(ARGV)
{
    WITH_BUILTIN_LIBPNG = yes ;
}

if ! $(WITH_BUILTIN_LIBPNG)
{
    if $(PREBUILT_ROOT)
    {
        PNG_INCLUDES  ?= [ Prebuilt-Includes png ] ;   # png + zlib includes
        PNG_C++FLAGS  ?= [ Prebuilt-CxxFlags png ] ;
        PNG_LINKLIBS  ?= [ Prebuilt-LinkLibs png ] ;   # png16 (+ syslibs)
        PNG_LIBDIR    ?= [ Prebuilt-LibDir png ] ;
        PNG_LINKFLAGS ?= [ Prebuilt-LinkFlags png ] ;
    }

    WITH_PNG = [ Png-Configure $(PNG_INCLUDES) : $(PNG_C++FLAGS) :
                               $(PNG_LINKLIBS) : $(PNG_LIBDIR) : $(PNG_LINKFLAGS) ] ;
}
```

Repeat for jpeg (`jpeg` → `JPEG_*`) and FreeType (`freetype` → `FREETYPE_*`).
`Prebuilt-Includes freetype` returns FreeType's include dirs plus png and
zlib, because `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS.freetype = png zlib`.

### 8.4 OpenSSL — same hints, framework prefix `SSL`

The zip package name is `openssl`. The Jam variables stay `SSL_*`, as
`OpenSsl-Configure` already uses.

`--with-openssl` (inline) and `--with-mbedtls` stay **above** the system /
prebuilt probe, as they do today. Only the `OpenSsl-Configure` branch takes
prebuilt hints:

```jam
Load openssl.jam ;

if --with-openssl in $(ARGV)
{
    include [ FDirName src openssl Jamconfigure ] ;
    WITH_OPENSSL = "yes" ;
    WITH_SSL = yes ;
    PT_SSL_IMPL = "openssl" ;
    SSL_INCLUDES = [ FDirName $(PT_TOP) src openssl include ] ;
}

# ... mbedtls, Apple SSL, unchanged ...

if ! $(WITH_SSL)
{
    if $(PREBUILT_ROOT)
    {
        SSL_INCLUDES  ?= [ Prebuilt-Includes openssl ] ;
        SSL_C++FLAGS  ?= [ Prebuilt-CxxFlags openssl ] ;
        SSL_LINKLIBS  ?= [ Prebuilt-LinkLibs openssl ] ;
        SSL_LIBDIR    ?= [ Prebuilt-LibDir openssl ] ;
        SSL_LINKFLAGS ?= [ Prebuilt-LinkFlags openssl ] ;
    }

    WITH_SSL = [ OpenSsl-Configure $(SSL_INCLUDES) : $(SSL_C++FLAGS) :
                                   $(SSL_LINKLIBS) : $(SSL_LIBDIR) : $(SSL_LINKFLAGS) ] ;
    if $(WITH_SSL)
    {
        PT_SSL_IMPL = "openssl" ;
    }
}
```

`Prebuilt-LinkLibs openssl` must return `ssl crypto` **and**
`crypt32 ws2_32 advapi32 user32` on MSVC, already passed through `FLinkLibs`.
Without `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS` on that hint line the MSVC probe fails even though
the import libs are present.

### 8.5 What Jamfiles do not change

`src/Pt-Gfx/Jamfile`, `src/Pt-Ssl/Jamfile`, `src/Pt/Jamfile` keep:

```jam
SubDirHdrs $(PNG_INCLUDES) ;
LinkFlags Pt-Gfx : $(PNG_LINKFLAGS) ;
LinkLibs  Pt-Gfx : $(PNG_LINKLIBS) ;
ObjectC++Flags PngReader.cpp PngWriter.cpp : $(PNG_C++FLAGS) ;
```

They never mention `PREBUILT_ROOT`. Builtin vs. prebuilt remains
`WITH_BUILTIN_LIBPNG` / `WITH_PNG` as today.

---

## 9. Deploy files

Deploy files are the `bin/` trees of the **chosen** ABI. Each package lists
its own files in `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY.$(pkg)` (names in `$(pkg)/bin`).

- `jam install` / app staging: copy `Prebuilt-Deploy` of the packages the
  app actually uses, closed over `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS`, next to the
  exe or into `PT_INSTALL_BINDIR`.
- Static `.lib` / `.a` in `lib/` do not ship with the binary.
- Transitive DLLs (brotli / bzip2 of FreeType) live in their own package
  directories in the prefix when the producer copied `cpp_info.bindirs`.

Windows: **`mdd` is not redistributable.** Debug CRT (`MSVCP*D.dll`,
`VCRUNTIME*D.dll`) is not in the VC redistributable, only on machines with
Visual Studio. Release products take `md`. Debug locally puts `mdd` DLLs next
to the exe. The framework treats the `mdd` identity as not redistributable;
the zip does not carry a `PREBUILT_REDIST` flag.

---

## 10. ABI contract and mixing project-debug with release deps

The prefix model always scales. Mixing project-debug against release deps
scales only under an ABI contract.

- C ABI, shared (zlib, png, jpeg, FreeType-C, OpenSSL):
  Linux/mac is the normal case. Windows `md` DLL vs. `/MDd` project is often
  fine because each DLL has its CRT — no heap / `FILE*` across the boundary.
- Static + different CRT:
  wrong on Windows.
- C++ with STL in the public API:
  `LNK2038` / `_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL`. Same ABI tag, or keep it out of the
  prefix.

Shared linkage and a C public ABI are a product decision, not extra
variables in `packages.jam` beyond `PREBUILT_LINKAGE`. A library that puts
`std::string` in its API is not a prebuilt candidate — or it forces the
same ABI tag (`mdd` with debug).

Three paths when C++ deps appear — not two zips "just in case" for zlib:

1. Vendor the heavy C++ pieces, same flags as the project.
2. A second ABI prefix only when the prebuilt actually splits (`mdd`, later
   `asan`).
3. Optional product choice: project-debug on Windows with `/MD /Od /Zi` —
   one prefix, not the de-facto VS debug ABI. Anyone who wants a normal VS
   debug session needs `mdd`.

Vendor remains the path that is **always** correct (static, templates,
exotics, "the same flags line by line").

---

## 11. Configure flags (framework side)

Three flags. Any one of them selects the prebuilt layer. Each may be bare
(the default) or take `=value`.

`--with-prebuilt` uses a prefix that is already on disk. `--fetch-prebuilt`
does the same configure, after fetching that prefix into the default cache.
The value of `--with-prebuilt` is the root when one is given.

`--with-prebuilt`

- Bare: detect `<os>-<arch>-<toolset>` and apply the ABI tag, then use
  `prebuilt/<id>/`. Error if that tree is missing. No network.
- `--with-prebuilt=windows-x64-msvc-md`: use that identity with
  `prebuilt/<id>/`. No network.

`--fetch-prebuilt`

- Same load as `--with-prebuilt` with the default cache, but download or
  refresh the zip first. Bare: detect identity, fetch into `prebuilt/<id>/`,
  then configure. This is the usual first-time command.
- `--fetch-prebuilt=windows-x64-msvc-md`: pin the identity to fetch.
- Implies the prebuilt layer; `--with-prebuilt` is not required next to it.
- Do not combine with `--with-prebuilt=<id>`. Fetch always targets the
  default cache; an explicit identity via `--with-prebuilt` is already
  local.

`--fetch-prebuilt` is not a boolean beside `--with-prebuilt`. A bool would
make the common case two flags (`--with-prebuilt --fetch-prebuilt`) and
would not give a place to pin identity. A stale default cache is refreshed
only by `--fetch-prebuilt`; `--with-prebuilt` will not phone home.

Not prebuilt-layer flags, unchanged, and still stronger than the layer:

- `--with-xxx-dir` / `ZLIB_INCLUDES=...` and the rest of the five-tuple:
  always win (`?=` does not overwrite)
- `--with-inline-xxx`: force vendor; skips `*-Configure`

Not used: `--with-prebuilt-root`, `--with-platform-sdk`, `--sdk-root`,
`--sdk-abi`, `--fetch-sdk`, `--no-fetch`.

Identity in the archive name and directory is `windows-x64-msvc-md` or
macos-arm64-clang`, not `x64-windows`. A single zip that contains both
`platform-sdk-release.jam` and `platform-sdk-debug.jam` is the debug/release
layout this design removes. One cache. Never mix `md` and `mdd` in one
search path.

---

## 12. Production (CI / Conan) — not the daily path

Conan exists only in the GitHub Action.

- Custom deployer (`sysroot_deploy`), not `full_deploy` as the shipping
  format.
- One directory per package: `$(pkg)/include`, `$(pkg)/lib`, `$(pkg)/bin`.
- Write `jam/packages.jam` from `cpp_info`: `libs` → `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS`,
  `requires` → `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS`, `system_libs` →
  `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_SYSLIBS`, `frameworks` → `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_FRAMEWORKS`,
  `bindirs` → `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPLOY`, extra `includedirs` →
  `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_INCLUDES`. The result is the same file a person would write.
- Do **not** emit `ZLIB_INCLUDES` / `PNG_LINKLIBS` from the generator. That
  mapping is `jam/prebuilt.jam`.
- Windows: two installs → two prefixes (or two zips): `...-md`, `...-mdd`.
- macOS / Linux: one untagged prefix until an ABI split exists.
- Assert: `zlib/include/zlib.h`, `zlib/lib/zlib.lib` or `libz.a`, `bin/`
  when shared, `jam/packages.jam`, **no** version tree, **no** absolute CI
  paths.
- Generator `.jam` with `-L` / `-l` and `SetDependencyPath` is not this path.

Default matrix: both Windows ABIs (the debug ABI is a prebuilt fact), one
untagged prefix for macOS. `mdd` is not a subfolder of the `md` zip.

---

## 13. Hand-written prefix / other build systems

By hand: fill the per-package directories, write `jam/packages.jam` with the
assignments, point `--with-prebuilt=` at it. No Conan.

Other systems: the file is `KEY = value` and
`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_LIBS.foo = ...`.
Parsable without executing Jam. No parallel `prebuilt.cmake` as source. Jam
stays canonical; a later mirror at most.

---

## 14. Deliberate non-goals

- `Prebuilt-Apply` / global `C++FLAGS +=` / global `LINKFLAGS +=`
- `SetDependencyPath` / `DEPS_INCLUDES` / `DEPS_LIBDIR` as the prebuilt
  interface
- Flat `include/` + `lib/` sysroot (rejected together with Apply)
- `debug/lib` inside the same prefix
- A second conceptual "debug layer" instead of an ABI tag
- Naming the only ABI `default` / `common` / `standard`
- Two-step Configure (system first, prebuilt second) while the prebuilt is
  selected
- `--with-prebuilt-root`; `--no-fetch`; `--fetch-prebuilt` as a boolean
  beside `--with-prebuilt`
- Writing `ZLIB_*` into the zip
- Per-package `package.jam` files or per-package zips
- Solving dependencies (versions, optionals, "find zlib elsewhere")
- Conan / vcpkg in the user build
- pkg-config as a requirement on Windows
- Raw flags in the zip (`-l`, `-L`, `-pthread`, `-framework`, `/LIBPATH`)
- Rules / `FLinkLibs` / `C++FLAGS` in `jam/packages.jam`
- A global `PREBUILT_DEPLOY_FILES` list
- Vendoring everything (OpenSSL in Jam: Perl / NASM)
- Forcing everything into the prefix (C++ STL API)
- CMake ExternalProject as a second build world

---

## 15. How the layers compose

```text
User flags / explicit five-tuple / --with-inline-xxx
        |
        |  else, if PREBUILT_ROOT is set
        v
Prebuilt hints  ->  one *-Configure probe
        |
        |  else (no prebuilt), or probe failed
        v
System / toolchain sysroot / pkg-config     <- Yocto, distro, manufacturer
        |                                     (only when the prebuilt was
        |                                      not selected)
        |  else
        v
Vendor / inline sources                     <- same flags as the project
```

When the prebuilt is selected, system is not consulted for packages the
prefix lists. When it is not selected, the prefix is not consulted at all.

Prebuilt: convenience for full prebuilts, including their extra ABIs.
Vendor: ABI-accurate path and cross.
System: when the machine already has the dependency.

---

## 16. Short form

**One layer. Prebuilts declare ABI variants; a single ABI is untagged.
Each ABI is a prefix of per-package directories plus `jam/packages.jam`
(`PREBUILT_*` identity, `PREBUILT_PACKAGE_*` maps). The consumer loads the
prefix, translates names with the toolset, and injects the existing
`*_INCLUDES` / `*_C++FLAGS` / `*_LINKLIBS` / `*_LIBDIR` / `*_LINKFLAGS`
hints — once, before `*-Configure`. Includes and deploy close over
`PREBUILT_PACKAGE_DEPENDS`; link lines close only for static. That walk is
not a solver. User overrides stay Configure. Deploy files are per package.
Conan stays in CI.**
